<!-- WHY VISIT -->
<section class="section"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-header reveal"><h2>Why Visit Huangshan</h2><div class="divider"></div></div>
  <div class="why-grid">
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#9968;</div><h3>Spectacular Scenery</h3><p>Rise above the clouds among Huangshan's legendary granite peaks, twisted ancient pines clinging impossibly to cliffs, and a sea of clouds that transforms the mountain into a floating celestial realm. Generations of Chinese painters and poets have tried to capture this beauty — now it's your turn.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#127758;</div><h3>UNESCO World Heritage</h3><p>Huangshan holds the rare honor of being both a UNESCO World Cultural and Natural Heritage Site since 1990. Its geological formations, biodiversity, and profound influence on Chinese landscape art and literature make it one of the most significant protected areas on Earth.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">🏠</div><h3>Hongcun &amp; Xidi Ancient Villages</h3><p>Descend from the peaks into the dreamy Huizhou-style villages of Hongcun and Xidi. Whitewashed walls, dark tiled roofs, moon-shaped ponds, and cobbled lanes reflect centuries of Ming and Qing dynasty architecture — scenes so perfect they inspired the film Crouching Tiger, Hidden Dragon.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#127748;</div><h3>Sunrise &amp; Sunset</h3><p>Watching the sun rise or set from Huangshan's summit is a spiritual experience. As golden light spills over the granite pinnacles and through the swirling mist, the sea of clouds glows in hues of pink, orange, and crimson — a view that has moved travelers for over a thousand years.</p></div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<!-- TOP ATTRACTIONS -->
<section class="section section-alt"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-header reveal"><h2>Top Attractions</h2><div class="divider"></div></div>
  <div class="tours-grid">
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/hero-silkroad.jpg" alt="Beginning-to-Believe Peak" width="1200" height="800"><div class="tour-badge">#1</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Beginning-to-Believe Peak</h3><p>This iconic peak is where visitors first truly grasp Huangshan's majesty. Its name comes from an ancient traveler who, upon reaching this spot, finally believed the legends about the mountain's beauty. The view of layered granite spires piercing through mist is the quintessential Huangshan photograph — and the perfect introduction to what awaits.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/hero-silkroad.jpg" alt="Bright Summit Peak" width="1200" height="800"><div class="tour-badge">#2</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Bright Summit Peak</h3><p>At 1,860 meters, Bright Summit Peak is one of Huangshan's three main peaks and offers the most commanding 360-degree panorama on the mountain. From here you can see both sunrise and sunset depending on the season, with views stretching across the entire granite range. The summit is accessible by cable car or via well-maintained stone steps.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/hero-silkroad.jpg" alt="West Sea Grand Canyon" width="1200" height="800"><div class="tour-badge">#3</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>West Sea Grand Canyon</h3><p>Known as the "Grand Canyon of Huangshan," this dramatic gorge plunges deep between sheer rock walls. A winding trail of stone steps — some carved directly into cliff faces — descends through fairy-tale scenery of pinnacles, pine trees, and swirling mist. The 3-4 hour hike is challenging but rewards with the mountain's most dramatic and least crowded vistas.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relat/assets/images/hero-silkroad.jpg" alt="Hongcun Ancient Village"><div class="tour-badge">#4</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Hongcun Ancient Village</h3><p>A UNESCO World Heritage village at the foot of Huangshan, Hongcun is a masterpiece of Huizhou architecture laid out in the shape of an ox. The crescent Moon Pond, the central South Lake, and white-walled ancestral halls create reflections so perfect the village appears painted on water. Every lane leads to a postcard-worthy scene — it's no wonder Ang Lee filmed here.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tour-card reveal">
      <div class="tour-image" style="position:relative;"><img loading="lazy" src="/assets/images/central-asia-architecture.jpg" alt="Huangshan Hot Springs" width="1200" height="800"><div class="tour-badge">#5</div></div>
      <div class="tour-body"><h3>Huangshan Hot Springs</h3><p>At the mountain's base, natural thermal springs have soothed travelers for centuries. The mineral-rich waters, bubbling up at around 42&deg;C, are the perfect antidote after a day of hiking steep stone steps. Legend says the Yellow Emperor himself bathed here before ascending to heaven — a tradition worth continuing on your visit.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<!-- BEST TIME TO VISIT -->
<section class="section"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-header reveal"><h2>Best Time to Visit</h2><div class="divider"></div></div>
  <div class="why-grid">
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#127800;</div><h3>Spring (Apr-May)</h3><p>The mountain awakens with blooming azaleas and wildflowers carpeting the slopes. Temperatures are mild (10-20&deg;C), and the misty weather creates dramatic cloud seas. April and May offer the best balance of comfortable hiking conditions and beautiful scenery — though rain is common, so pack waterproof gear.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#127809;</div><h3>Autumn (Sep-Nov)</h3><p>The golden season — crisp, clear air with brilliant blue skies that make the granite peaks pop against the horizon. Temperatures range from 10-22&deg;C, and the autumn foliage adds warm reds and golds to the landscape. September and October are widely considered the best months for photography, with the highest chance of clear summit views.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#127774;</div><h3>Summer (Jun-Aug)</h3><p>Crowded but lush — the mountain is at its greenest with waterfalls flowing at full force. Temperatures at the summit are a pleasant 15-25&deg;C, offering welcome relief from the lowland heat. Expect more visitors, especially during July and August school holidays. Afternoon thunderstorms are common but pass quickly, often leaving behind spectacular rainbows.</p></div>
    <div class="why-card reveal"><div class="why-icon">&#10052;</div><h3>Winter (Dec-Mar)</h3><p>Huangshan transformed into a snow-dusted wonderland — granite peaks and pine branches coated in rime ice create an ethereal monochrome landscape. Temperatures dip to -5 to 5&deg;C, but the views of a frozen sea of clouds are truly magical. Fewer crowds mean you'll have the trails largely to yourself, and hotels offer significant winter discounts.</p></div>
  </div>
</div></section>

